WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.108
Client-side validation is valuable for usability.

00:00:03.108 --> 00:00:10.776
It can catch missing fields, obvious formatting errors, and range problems before a user submits a form.

00:00:10.776 --> 00:00:18.857
Browser controls, frontend libraries, and shared schemas can make interfaces feel responsive and reduce unnecessary server round trips.

00:00:18.857 --> 00:00:23.624
But client-side validation is not a security control by itself.

00:00:23.624 --> 00:00:27.975
Browsers and clients can be modified, bypassed, or replaced.

00:00:27.975 --> 00:00:39.373
A user can send requests directly to an API, change hidden fields, alter JavaScript in the browser, replay a request, or build a different client entirely.

00:00:39.373 --> 00:00:47.454
For that reason, server-side validation must enforce the real rules before processing, storing, or acting on untrusted data.

00:00:47.454 --> 00:00:50.977
Shared schemas can reduce duplicated inconsistent rules.

00:00:50.977 --> 00:00:58.230
A team may use the same schema definition, or generated models, across frontend and backend code.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:04.654
This can improve consistency, but it does not eliminate the need for server enforcement.

00:01:04.654 --> 00:01:11.078
The server should assume that any client-side check may have been skipped or altered.

00:01:11.078 --> 00:01:14.186
Error messages should be designed carefully.

00:01:14.186 --> 00:01:23.097
They should help legitimate users fix input problems without exposing stack traces, database structure, secret values, or unnecessary implementation details.

00:01:23.097 --> 00:01:36.152
The same principle applies to logs: record enough information to debug and monitor invalid input, but avoid storing raw sensitive values or payloads that could make logs risky to handle.
